Here are clean copy-paste steps for all questions:

```
=== CCE 5253 Cloud Security Essentials Lab - End Semester Exam ===

--- Q1 Write-up: Principle of Least Privilege (10 marks) ---
Write the following points:
- Definition: Give users/services only the minimum permissions needed to do their job
- No extra access beyond what is required
- Reduces attack surface if account is compromised
- Example: A developer only gets S3 read/write, not admin access
- AWS implementation: IAM policies, roles, and permission boundaries enforce this
- Regular audits should be done to remove unused permissions

-------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Q2: AWS IAM Setup for ABCDocs (15 marks) ---

STEP 1 - Create IAM Groups:
1. IAM → User groups → Create group → Name: Developers → Create
2. IAM → User groups → Create group → Name: Auditors → Create

STEP 2 - Create IAM Users:
1. IAM → Users → Add users → Name: Dev-user → Next
   Select group: Developers → Next → Create user
2. IAM → Users → Add users → Name: Audit-user → Next
   Select group: Auditors → Next → Create user

STEP 3 - Configure Permissions:
1. IAM → User groups → Developers → Permissions → Add permissions
   → Attach policies → search "S3" → select AmazonS3FullAccess → Add
2. IAM → User groups → Auditors → Permissions → Add permissions
   → Attach policies → search "S3" → select AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess → Add

STEP 4 - Configure IAM Password Policy:
1. IAM → Account settings → Edit password policy
2. Minimum password length: 10
3. Check: Require numbers
4. Check: Require symbols
5. Save changes

STEP 5 - Enable MFA for IAM Users:
1. IAM → Users → Dev-user → Security credentials → Assign MFA device
2. Choose: Authenticator app → Next → scan QR with phone app → enter 2 codes → Add MFA
3. Repeat same steps for Audit-user

STEP 6 - Demonstrate Login:
1. Copy the IAM sign-in URL from IAM Dashboard
2. Open browser → paste URL → login with Dev-user credentials
3. Show S3 access works
4. Login with Audit-user → show read-only access

-------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Q3: CloudTrail + CloudWatch Monitoring for ABCDocs (15 marks) ---

STEP 1 - Enable AWS CloudTrail:
1. Go to CloudTrail → Create trail
2. Trail name: ABCDocs-trail
3. Apply to all regions: Yes → Next → Create trail

STEP 2 - Store CloudTrail Logs in S3:
1. During trail creation → S3 bucket: Create new → name: abcdocs-cloudtrail-logs
2. (This is auto-configured when creating the trail)

STEP 3 - Create CloudWatch Alarms:

  Alarm A - EC2 CPU > 80%:
  1. CloudWatch → Alarms → Create alarm → Select metric
  2. EC2 → Per-Instance Metrics → CPUUtilization → Select metric
  3. Threshold: Greater than 80 → Next
  4. Notification: Create new SNS topic (or use existing) → Next → Name: CPU-High-Alarm → Create

  Alarm B - High Network Traffic:
  1. CloudWatch → Alarms → Create alarm → Select metric
  2. EC2 → Per-Instance Metrics → NetworkIn → Select metric
  3. Threshold: set a high value (e.g. 10000000 bytes) → Next
  4. Attach SNS topic → Next → Name: Network-High-Alarm → Create

STEP 4 - Create SNS Topic and Add Email:
1. Go to SNS → Topics → Create topic
2. Type: Standard → Name: ABCDocs-Alerts → Create topic
3. Subscriptions → Create subscription
4. Protocol: Email → Endpoint: your-email@example.com → Create
5. Go to your email → confirm the subscription link

STEP 5 - Link CloudWatch Alarms to SNS:
1. CloudWatch → Alarms → open CPU-High-Alarm → Edit
2. Notification → In alarm → select ABCDocs-Alerts SNS topic → Update alarm
3. Repeat for Network-High-Alarm

-------------------------------------------------------------------

Q4 - Viva (10 marks): Be ready to explain what you did in Q2 and Q3
```
